Title: Advanced Effect Handlers — Koka Topic Guide
[INCLUDE=../sidebar-sub.mdk]

# Advanced Effect Handlers { .heading-no-number }

This guide covers advanced techniques for working with effect handlers in Koka,
including side-effect isolation, resource control, and scoped handlers.

---

## Side-effect Isolation { #sec-isolate }

One of the strengths of Koka's effect system is the ability to strictly isolate state.
The `runst` function represents isolated state (reference cells). When a stateful
computation completes without returning any stateful references, it is considered pure.

---

## Duplicate Labels and `catch` Typing { #sec-duplicate-labels }

Duplicate effect labels are sometimes intentional. A common place this shows up is when
building reusable higher-order handler combinators like `catch`:

```koka
effect raise
  ctl raise( msg : string ) : a

fun catch( hnd : (string) -> e a, action : () -> <raise|e> a ) : e a
  with ctl raise(msg) hnd(msg)
  action()
```

If another `raise` handler is already in scope, the inner one handles the closest `raise`
first, and the outer one remains available. Thinking in terms of a handler stack makes this
behavior predictable and explains why duplicate labels are not inherently ambiguous.

---

## Multiple Resumptions { #sec-multi-resume }

Since `resume` is a first-class function (well, almost, see [raw control](#sec-rcontrol)),
it is possible to invoke it more than once. This can be used to
implement backtracking or probabilistic programming models.

A common example of multiple resumptions is the `:choice` effect:

```koka
effect ctl choice() : bool

fun xor() : choice bool
  val p = choice()
  val q = choice()
  if p then !q else q
```

We can resume multiple times, once with `False` and once with `True`,
to return *all* possible outcomes:

```koka
fun choice-all(action : () -> <choice|e> a) : e list<a>
  with handler
    return(x)    [x]
    ctl choice() resume(False) ++ resume(True)
  action()
```

---

## Masking Effects { #sec-mask }

Use `mask<l>` when you need to skip the innermost handler for label `l` and target the next
handler outwards. In nested handlers for the same label, this gives explicit control over where
an operation is routed.

```unchecked
effect fun emit( msg : string ) : ()

fun emit-quoted( action : () -> <emit|e> a ) : <emit|e> a
  with fun emit(msg) emit("\"" ++ msg ++ "\"")
  action()

fun emit-raw-outside() : <emit|e> ()
  with fun emit(msg) println("outer: " ++ msg)
  with emit-quoted
  // Skip the innermost emit handler, go directly to the outer one.
  mask<emit>(emit("hello"))
```

Type-wise, `mask<l>` can expose duplicate labels because one handler is skipped while another
for the same label remains in scope. This is one of the few places where duplicate labels are
an intentional part of the design.

Masking is useful but should stay rare: most code should use ordinary handler abstraction first.
Reach for `mask` when writing low-level handler libraries where routing to a specific outer
handler is required.

---

## Effect Abstraction with Masking { #sec-mask-abstraction }

Masking can hide internal effect management from clients of higher-order helpers.
Conceptually, a `mask-print` helper can catch internal exceptions while preserving a clean outer
effect surface for the caller.

```unchecked
fun mask-print( doc : string, action : () -> <raise,emit|e> () ) : <emit|e> ()
  with ctl raise(msg)
    emit(doc ++ ": " ++ msg)
    ()
  // Forward emits to outer context, but avoid exposing internal raise handling details.
  mask<emit>(action())
```

Without masking in this style of abstraction, handled effects can leak into public types of
higher-order utilities.

---

## State as a Combined Effect { #sec-state-combined }

State can be modeled directly from handlers, where reads and writes are routed through explicit
operations and masking controls which state layer is addressed.

```unchecked
effect ctl peek() : a
effect ctl poke(x:a) : ()

fun state-via-handlers( init : a, action : () -> <peek,poke|e> b ) : e (b,a)
  var st := init
  with handler
    return(x)    (x,st)
    ctl peek()   resume(st)
    ctl poke(x)  st := x; resume(())
  action()
```

This perspective is useful for understanding semantics and composition, but it can also create
deeper handler stacks in heavily abstracted code. In practice, prefer direct handlers first and
introduce masking/state encodings when you specifically need their extra expressiveness.

---

## `mask behind` and `override` Internals { #sec-mask-behind }

`override` is the ergonomic way to replace an existing handler. At a lower level, this behavior
cannot be reproduced modularly with plain `mask` alone; it requires a primitive often described
as `mask behind`, which routes an operation behind the current handler frame.

Most users should treat this as implementation detail:

- Use `override` in normal code.
- Use `mask` for selective routing to an outer handler.
- Reserve `mask behind` concepts for low-level handler-library design.

---

## Initially and Finally { #sec-resource }

With arbitrary effect handlers we need to be careful when interacting with external
resources like files. Operations can never resume (like exceptions),
resume exactly once (giving the usual linear control flow), or resume more than once.

To robustly handle these different cases, Koka provides the `finally` and `initially`
functions. `finally` takes a function that is executed when returning normally, or
when unwinding for a non-resuming operation:

```koka
fun test-finally()
  with finally{ println("exiting..") }
  println("entering..")
  throw("oops") + 42
```

Running `test-finally()` prints:

```unchecked
entering..
exiting..
uncaught exception: oops
```

This is a nice example of Koka's "minimal but general" style: where other languages add a
special `defer` construct, Koka can express the same cleanup pattern as an ordinary function
used with `with`.

```unchecked
type fhandle
fun fopen( path : string )   : <exn,filesys> fhandle
fun hreadline( h : fhandle ) : <exn,filesys> string
fun hclose( h : fhandle )    : <exn,filesys> ()
effect fun fread() : string

// Robust file handling with finally
fun with-file( path : string, action : () -> <fread,exn,filesys|e> a ) : <exn,filesys|e> a
  val h = fopen(path)
  with finally
    hclose(h)
  with fun fread()
    hreadline(h)
  action()
```

Another way to work with multiple resumptions is to use the `initially` function,
which is called the first time it is evaluated, and subsequently every time a resumption
is resumed more than once.

---

## Raw Control { #sec-rcontrol }

Use `raw ctl` for raw control operations which do not automatically
finalize. With `raw ctl` one can use the implicitly bound
resumption context `rcontext` to either resume (as `rcontext.resume(x)`),
or to finalize a resumption (as `rcontext.finalize`) which runs all
`finally` handlers to clean up resources.

This allows one to store an `rcontext` as a first class value to resume or finalize later
even from a different scope.

---

## Linear Effects { #sec-linear }

Use `linear effect` to declare effects whose operations are always tail-resumptive
and use only linear effects themselves (and thus resume exactly once).
This removes monadic translation for such effects and can make code that uses
only linear effects more compact and efficient.

---

## Named and Scoped Handlers { #sec-namedh }

Koka supports named and scoped handlers for distinguishing identical effects.
See the `samples/handlers/named` directory for examples.

